## Project Summary

Saga is a local-first reading app for long-form nonfiction, built for committed readers who want to *finish* what they start and get value back out of what they highlight. It is a product surface (earned familiarity over novelty): the interface recedes so the text can do the work. The core loop is read → capture in one gesture → return to your marks.

## Product context

- **Register:** product — a daily reading tool; the chrome disappears into the reading.
- **Primary user:** committed nonfiction readers (15-40 books/year) who outgrew Kindle and read-it-later apps and keep highlights by hand.
- **Jobs-to-be-done:** finish the long thing across devices; capture a passage in one gesture; return to marks and act on them.
- **Personality (three words):** literary, unobtrusive, exacting. Sounds like a well-edited book, never a productivity coach.
- **Top anti-references:** Kindle (closed annotations), Goodreads (reading as performance), Duolingo-style gamification.

## UX laws

- **Recognition over recall** — always show the exact place ("p.42, *Chapter 3*").
- **Undo over confirm** — destructive actions show an inline "Undo," never an "Are you sure?" modal.
- **Progressive disclosure** — default surface is just text; notes, tags, export reveal on demand.
- **Keyboard-first power flows** — arrows turn pages, `h` highlights, `/` searches Marks.
- **Empty states are onboarding** — heading, one sentence, and the primary action inline.
- **Speak the reader's language** — "page," "chapter," "highlight," never "session."
- **Accessibility is built-in** — WCAG 2.1 AA, reader-controlled type/measure, true low-light dark theme, visible focus, `prefers-reduced-motion` honored.
- **Top flows stay cheap** — adding a tap to "continue" or "capture," or dropping a highlight anchor on re-import, is a regression.

## Design laws

- **Color: Restrained** — warm OKLCH paper neutrals + a single terracotta accent on ≤10% of surface. No pure black/white. Accent for marks/active state only.
- **Typography: editorial serif** — Fraunces display, Spectral body at 18px, 1.25 scale, 1.6 body line-height, measure capped at 68ch.
- **Spacing & layout** — 4px unit, airy single reading column. **No cards in the Reader.** Group with spacing and hairlines, never nested surfaces.
- **Motion: considered** — expo-out, 120/200/320ms; page turns cross-fade, never flip. Animate only `transform`/`opacity`; instant cut under `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- **Components** — sharp 2px corners, no resting shadows; surfaces separated by hairlines and whitespace.

## Code conventions

- **Stack:** Vite + React 18 (TS) SPA; Fastify sync+parse API; SQLite/Turso server + IndexedDB client (local-first); Fly.io; magic-link auth; EPUB.js/pdf.js import pipeline.
- **Architecture:** logic on the client, server is sync + parser. Source of truth in IndexedDB, synced to Turso. State in URL (location), Zustand (session), IndexedDB (persistence). Code grouped by feature.
- **Tooling:** TypeScript strict, pnpm, Biome, Vite, Vitest, Playwright.
- **Naming/structure:** kebab-case files, named exports only, feature folders, ordered imports. No `utils.ts` dumping ground.
- **Comments:** only when the *why* is non-obvious. **Errors:** propagate to the feature boundary and become user-facing messages.
- **Performance (hard limits):** LCP < 2.5s, INP < 200ms, CLS < 0.1; initial route JS < 150KB gzipped; parsers lazy-loaded off the reading path; page turn paints < 100ms.
- **Security:** secrets via env; single-use magic-link tokens; validate at the boundary; parse uploaded files with no network access; CSP enforced. Treat every imported file as hostile.

## Anti-patterns (always reject)

- **Product:** vague personas; generic positioning; educating an aware audience; hedging brand voice; feature-lists-as-positioning; ornamental metrics; unreal anti-references; buzzword personality.
- **UX:** confirm-instead-of-undo; modal-first; hidden state; empty states without guidance; generic errors; inconsistent validation; sub-44px touch targets; dark patterns; lossy navigation; accessibility-as-cleanup; tour onboarding; recall-over-recognition.
- **Design:** purple-on-dark/neon/pure-black-white/multi-accent/gray-on-colored; system-only type, line-height <1.4, measure >75ch, scale <1.2, gradient text, long all-caps; nested cards, identical-card grids, hero-metric, side-stripes, default shadows, glassmorphism; bounce easing, layout-property animation, reduced-motion violations, delight on critical paths; icon-headline-paragraph grids, "how it works" columns, logo strips, tilted-screenshot heroes.
- **Code:** premature abstraction; half-finished stubs; defensive-over-trust; comment-as-rename; dead code; backward-compat shims for owned code; over-eager error handling; boolean state machines; test-by-implementation; snapshot-as-primary; grab-bag utility files; silent type widening; magic timing; bypassing safety.

## Project-specific notes

- The **import pipeline** (`import/`, EPUB.js + pdf.js) is the riskiest surface — plan before changing it, and never run parsers with network access.
- The **highlight anchor-matcher** and **sync merge** logic must keep their tests green; treat a dropped highlight anchor on re-import as a release blocker.
- Reading-path performance is a hard limit: keep parsers lazy-loaded, never import EPUB.js/pdf.js on the Reader route.
- The codebase is grouped by feature (`library/`, `reader/`, `marks/`, `import/`, `sync/`).
